<section class="n-bg-white" aria-label="Introduction">
<div class="n-wrap" style="padding-top:clamp(28px,3.5vw,56px);padding-bottom:clamp(32px,4vw,64px)">
<div class="n-read">
<p class="n-lead" style="margin:0">Niwot exists because a rail line was built through the Left Hand valley in the 1870s. The town plat was recorded in 1875, and the street grid still follows the tracks rather than the county roads that came later. The brick block on Second Avenue is what remains of that decision.</p>
<p class="n-body" style="margin:22px 0 0">It has never been an incorporated municipality. There is no mayor and no town hall, which is part of why the place feels the way it does: what happens here is organized by the people who live here, through the business association, the arts groups and the neighbors who show up.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="n-bg-paper n-pad" aria-labelledby="name-h">
<div class="n-wrap">
<div class="n-shead" style="border-bottom-color:var(--n-evergreen)">
<div class="n-label">The name</div>
<h2 class="n-h2" id="name-h" style="color:var(--n-evergreen);max-width:24ch">Niwot is named for Chief Niwot.</h2>
</div>
<div class="n-read" style="padding-top:clamp(20px,2.5vw,34px)">
<p class="n-body" style="margin:0">Chief Niwot was a leader of the Southern Arapaho, whose people lived in and travelled through this part of the Front Range long before the town site was surveyed. His name, rendered in English as &#8220;left hand,&#8221; is carried by the creek that crosses the valley, by the water district, and by the community itself.</p>
<p class="n-body" style="margin:20px 0 0">In November 1864, Colorado volunteer cavalry attacked an encampment of Cheyenne and Arapaho people at Sand Creek, killing mostly women, children and elders. Chief Niwot was among those wounded there and died shortly afterward. The Sand Creek Massacre is recognized today as a massacre by the United States government, and it is inseparable from the history of the name this community uses.</p>
<p class="n-body" style="margin:20px 0 0">That history is not a decorative origin story, and a community guide is not its proper author. The account above is deliberately brief and points to the nations and institutions that hold it.</p>
